{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | nl -ba | sed -n '63,240p'\"","aggregated_output":"    63\t### 1.3 Fields\n    64\t\n    65\tA field is a key of the row, or an object when it needs more. The key is checked against the row type by role: a value axis accepts number fields; text fields are accepted there only with `parse: 'number'`.\n    66\t\n    67\t```ts\n    68\tline(el, {\n    69\t  data: samples,\n    70\t  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' },\n    71\t  y: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'sessions', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n    72\t});\n    73\t\n    74\t// counts that a database driver returns as strings\n    75\tbar(el, { data: pgRows, x: 'day', y: { field: 'count', parse: 'number', missing: 'zero' } });\n    76\t```\n    77\t\n    78\t| Option | Meaning |\n    79\t|---|---|\n    80\t| `field` | Key of the row. |\n    81\t| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n    82\t| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n    83\t| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n    84\t| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n    85\t| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n    86\t| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n    87\t| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n    88\t| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n    89\t\n    90\t### 1.4 Common options\n    91\t\n    92\tSummarized; the declarations are the reference.\n    93\t\n    94\t| Option | Purpose |\n    95\t|---|---|\n    96\t| `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description` | The chart's visible heading and accessible name (A1). `title` may carry a heading `level` and `visible: false`; `labelledBy` points at a heading the page already has. |\n    97\t| `state` | `'ready'`, `'loading'`, `{ error }`, `{ empty }` (L7). |\n    98\t| `legend`, `tooltip`, `table` | On by default; each can be turned off. |\n    99\t| `zoom`, `reference` | Cartesian charts only. |\n   100\t| `compact` | Line, area and bar: marks only, as a static image with a text alternative (spec 3.7). |\n   101\t| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n   102\t| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\n   103\t| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\n   104\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 1.7 React adapter (not in 1.0)\n   150\t\n   151\tRemoved from the first release by the owner on 2026-11-18: 1.0 is plain TypeScript, and adapters for React, Vue, Svelte and others follow later. The adapter described below was built, reviewed twice and fixed; its last state is `packages/react` at commit dac5664. The section is kept as the design to return to.\n   152\t\n   153\t```tsx\n   154\timport { Line } from '@axylo/react';\n   155\t\n   156\t<Line data={rows} x=\"day\" y=\"sessions\" series=\"channel\" title=\"Daily sessions by channel\" />\n   157\t```\n   158\t\n   159\t| Behavior | Detail |\n   160\t|---|---|\n   161\t| Lifecycle | Create on mount, `destroy` on unmount; correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup, setup. The file is marked `'use client'`. |\n   162\t| Props to options | Field specs are compared structurally, so an inline `x={{ field: 'ts' }}` does not renormalize on every render. `data` is compared by identity; in development, the same content arriving three times with a new identity raises a hint to memoize it. |\n   163\t| Removed props | A prop that disappears is sent as `undefined`, which restores the default. |\n   164\t| Handlers | Replaced without recreating the chart. |\n   165\t| Streaming | A ref exposes the handle, so `append`, `zoom` and `diagnostics` are reachable. When `data` is controlled, a new `data` prop replaces whatever was appended. |\n   166\t\n   167\tThe adapter is prototyped in M3, before the API freezes, because it is the cheapest way to find out whether the core contract survives a second consumer. It ships in 1.0 because finding 1.1 of the landscape shows where everyday charts are written; that is evidence of where charts are written, not of demand for Axylo.\n   168\t\n   169\t## 2. Architecture\n   170\t\n   171\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   172\t\n   173\t```\n   174\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   175\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   176\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   177\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   178\t                                         └─► focus model\n   179\t```\n   180\t\n   181\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   182\t\n   183\t| Stage | Output | Notes |\n   184\t|---|---|---|\n   185\t| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n   186\t| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n   187\t| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n   188\t| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n   189\t| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n   190\t| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n   191\t\n   192\t### 2.2 Invalidation\n   193\t\n   194\tStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n   195\t\n   196\t| Stage | Reruns when |\n   197\t|---|---|\n   198\t| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n   199\t| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n   200\t| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n   201\t| scene | Layout or view model. |\n   202\t| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n   203\t\n   204\tText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n   205\t\n   206\t### 2.3 Rendering\n   207\t\n   208\t**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n   209\t\n   210\t**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n   211\t\n   212\tM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n   213\t\n   214\t**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n   215\t\n   216\t**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n   217\t\n   218\t### 2.4 Document structure\n   219\t\n   220\t```html\n   221\t<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n   222\t  <figcaption>\n   223\t    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n   224\t    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n   225\t  </figcaption>\n   226\t  <div class=\"axylo-plot\" tabindex=\"0\"> … marks … <div class=\"axylo-text\" aria-hidden=\"true\">tick labels</div></div>\n   227\t  <div class=\"axylo-toolbar\" role=\"toolbar\" aria-label=\"Chart controls\">   <!-- one tab stop, roving focus -->\n   228\t    legend toggles · zoom in · zoom out · reset · pause · view as table\n   229\t  </div>\n   230\t  <p class=\"axylo-note\" role=\"status\">Values not shown: 2. See table.</p>\n   231\t  <div class=\"axylo-live\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n   232\t  <div class=\"axylo-tooltip\" hidden></div>\n   233\t  <div class=\"axylo-table\" hidden><!-- built when opened: caption, headers, pager --></div>\n   234\t</figure>\n   235\t```\n   236\t\n   237\tIds are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n   238\t\n   239\tDefault styles are applied without injecting a `<style>` element, and a standalone stylesheet ships for pages that prefer to load it themselves. Which mechanism passes a strict Content Security Policy is settled by the M7 test with real headers, not assumed.\n   240\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
