rowser/chart/late-font.browser.test.ts\nA\tpackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/loadingdone.browser.test.ts\nA\tpackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/reattach.browser.test.ts\nA\tpackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/scale.browser.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/browser/harness.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/core/final-review.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/core/focus-placement.test.ts\nA\tpackages/axylo/test/core/focus-range.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/core/focus-reading.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/core/host.ts\nA\tpackages/axylo/test/core/verify-pass.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/data/streaming-storage.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/layout/layout.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/lookup/aligned.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/lookup/donut.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/lookup/scatter.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/scene/scene.test.ts\nM\tpackages/axylo/test/theme/palette.test.ts\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/02-product-spec.md | nl -ba | sed -n '76,234p'\"","aggregated_output":"    76\t### 2.3 Supported environments\n    77\t\n    78\t| Dimension | 1.0 |\n    79\t|---|---|\n    80\t| Browsers | The current and previous major versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari (macOS and iOS). |\n    81\t| TypeScript | 5.4 and later. Plain JavaScript is fully supported; types are an aid, not a requirement. |\n    82\t| Frameworks | None needed. No adapter ships in 1.0; the library is called from any framework's mount and unmount hooks. |\n    83\t| Text direction | Left-to-right pages. Right-to-left text renders, but the layout is not mirrored; this is stated in the conformance table. |\n    84\t| Transformed ancestors | A chart inside a translated or scaled ancestor (any positive scale, uniform or not) is exact. Inside a rotated, skewed or mirrored ancestor it draws, but pointer picking and tooltip placement are wrong; this is not supported in 1.0 and the documentation says so (decision 0004, rule 10). |\n    85\t| Built-in text | English, overridable (A10). |\n    86\t\n    87\t## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n    90\t\n    91\t### 3.1 Faithful representation\n    92\t\n    93\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n    94\t|---|---|---|---|\n    95\t| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n    96\t| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n    97\t| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n    98\t| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n    99\t| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n   100\t| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n   101\t| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. This rule is for value axes: the y axis of line, area and bar, and both axes of scatter. A continuous x axis of line, area or bar spans exactly its data (plus half a band for bars), so a time series is not padded to the next tick. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n   102\t| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n   103\t| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars, full and compact alike: color and fill pattern; for every chart type at least 32 distinct combinations, after which combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n   104\t| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n   105\t| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n   106\t| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n   107\t\n   108\t### 3.2 Legibility\n   109\t\n   110\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   111\t|---|---|---|---|\n   112\t| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n   113\t| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n   114\t| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n   115\t| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n   116\t| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n   117\t| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n   118\t| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n   119\t| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n   120\t\n   121\t### 3.3 Interaction\n   122\t\n   123\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   124\t|---|---|---|---|\n   125\t| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot; where even the strip would leave the plot under the minimum of L8, pointing highlights the datum without a readout and the values are in the keyboard reading and the table. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n   126\t| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n   127\t| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n   128\t| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n   129\t| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n   130\t| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n   131\t\n   132\t### 3.4 Accessibility\n   133\t\n   134\tTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n   135\t\n   136\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   137\t|---|---|---|---|\n   138\t| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n   139\t| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n   140\t| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n   141\t| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n   142\t| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n   143\t| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n   144\t| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n   145\t| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n   146\t| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n   147\t| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n   148\t\n   149\t### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n   150\t\n   151\tLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n   152\t\n   153\t| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n   154\t|---|---|---|---|\n   155\t| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n   156\t| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n   157\t| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n   158\t| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n   159\t| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n   160\t| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n   161\t| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n   162\t| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n   163\t| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n   164\t| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n   165\t| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n   166\t| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n   167\t| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n   168\t| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n   169\t| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n   170\t| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n   171\t| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n   172\t| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n   173\t| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n   174\t| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n   175\t| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n   176\t| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n   177\t| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n   178\t| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n   179\t| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n   180\t| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n   181\t| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n   182\t| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n   183\t| AX199 | Error | Axylo itself failed while building the chart | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n   184\t\n   185\tExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n   186\t\n   187\tThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n   188\t\n   189\t### 3.6 Type matrix\n   190\t\n   191\t| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n   192\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   193\t| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n   194\t| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n   195\t| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n   196\t| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n   197\t| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n   198\t| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n   199\t| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n   200\t\n   201\t### 3.7 Compact variant\n   202\t\n   203\t`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n   204\t\n   205\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n   206\t|---|---|---|\n   207\t| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n   208\t| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n   209\t| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n   210\t| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n   211\t| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n   212\t\n   213\tA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n   214\t\n   215\t### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n   216\t\n   217\tAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n   218\t\n   219\t## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n   220\t\n   221\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   222\t|---|---|---|---|\n   223\t| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n   224\t| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n   225\t| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n   226\t| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n   227\t| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n   228\t| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n   229\t| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 96 KiB gzipped for everything and 80 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (The first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; an analysis of the built library showed they cannot hold the accessibility layer, the table, diagnostics, three kinds of time and streaming: `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n   230\t| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n   231\t| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n   232\t| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n   233\t| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n   234\t| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
